.TH GPIOD 4
.SH NAME
gpiod \- the GPIO server: a Raspberry Pi's pins (port "gpio")
.SH SYNOPSIS
Port
.BR gpio .
.B /bin/gpiod
is started on demand by
.BR init (8),
with device rights, by the first program that looks up "gpio", then kept (a
pattern may be playing).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B gpiod
offers the pins to every program, and plays patterns on them with steady
timing.
Pins are numbered as the chip does: a Raspberry Pi's BCM numbers, 0 to 27 on
the 40-pin header.
On a Raspberry Pi the pins are real: the Pi 2 to 4's GPIO block, or the Pi
5's, in its RP1 chip behind PCIe.
Elsewhere (x86-64, QEMU) they are simulated, so programs and tests run the
same everywhere: an output reads back its level, an input reads its pull.
.PP
A pin is an input (with a pull-up, a pull-down or none) or an output (0 or
1).
A pin a device uses (the serial console's 14 and 15, I2C, SPI) is shown as
busy and refused.
A pattern is a list of steps, each the levels of the pins it drives, one
step every step_us microseconds, once or looping, played by the server's own
thread (deadlines from the start, so the timing does not drift); it records
every pin's level at each step.
Programs use the C library
.BR <gpio.h> .
.SH MESSAGES
.TP
.B GPIO_INFO
\-> the number of pins; reply w[1] = 1: real pins, 0: simulated; rbuf <\- the
board's name.
.TP
.B GPIO_MODE
w[1] = pin, w[2] = GPIO_IN or GPIO_OUT, w[3] = GPIO_PULL_NONE, _DOWN or _UP
\-> 0.
.TP
.BR GPIO_WRITE ", " GPIO_READ
w[1] = pin, w[2] = 0 or 1 (an output) \-> 0; \-> the level.
.TP
.B GPIO_STATE
rbuf <\- gpio_pin_t[28] (mode, pull, level) \-> the levels, a bit per pin.
.TP
.B GPIO_PLAY
sbuf = gpio_pattern_t (mask, steps, step_us, loop) then the steps (a 32-bit
word of levels each, at most 4096) \-> 0.
.TP
.BR GPIO_STOP ", " GPIO_PLAYING
stop the pattern (the pins stay as they are); \-> the step being played (\-1:
none), reply w[1] = rounds done.
.TP
.B GPIO_RECORD
rbuf <\- every pin's level at each step of the pattern as it played \-> steps.
.TP
.B GPIO_SELFTEST
check every free pin, nothing wired to it, then put each back as it was;
rbuf <\- a byte per pin (GPIO_CHK_...) \-> the pins that failed. \-EBUSY while
a pattern plays.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gpio (1)
